    XAML Engine (How It Really Works Internally)
    This is the foundation for understanding:
    • Binding
    • Templates
    • Resources
    • Dependency Properties
    • Design tools
    • WPF’s dynamic UI

    XAML is not just XML.
    It is a markup language that maps directly to .NET objects.

    You write:
    &lt;Button Content="Click me" Width="100"/&gt;

    WPF must convert this XML into:
    Button btn = new Button();
    btn.Content = "Click me";
    btn.Width = 100;

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    1. During Build — XAML becomes BAML
    When you compile:
    MyWindow.xaml
        → MyWindow.g.cs (auto-generated)
        → MyWindow.baml (Binary XAML)

    What is BAML?
    • Binary Application Markup Language
    • Highly compressed
    • Much faster to load than raw XAML
    • Stored inside assembly as a resource

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    2. At Runtime — BAML Loader Creates Your UI Tree
    WPF performs the following steps:

    ✔ XAML parser reads BAML  
    ✔ Creates .NET objects (Button, Grid, Border…)  
    ✔ Applies dependency property values  
    ✔ Applies styles  
    ✔ Applies templates  
    ✔ Builds Logical Tree  
    ✔ Builds Visual Tree  
    ✔ Sends visuals to rendering pipeline  

    This process completes before your Window appears.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    3. XAML = Object Instantiation + Property Setting
    Example:
    &lt;Button&gt;
        &lt;Button.Background&gt;
            &lt;SolidColorBrush Color="Red"/&gt;
        &lt;/Button.Background&gt;
    &lt;/Button&gt;

    Internally becomes:
    Button btn = new Button();
    btn.Background = new SolidColorBrush(Colors.Red);

    Every XAML tag = a .NET class
    Every attribute = a property setter

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    4. Markup Extensions (Critical Feature)
    XAML supports “special syntax” through markup extensions:

    Examples:
    Text="{Binding Name}"
    Background="{StaticResource MyBrush}"
    ToolTip="{DynamicResource HighlightColor}"

    What handles this?
    • BindingExtension  
    • StaticResourceExtension  
    • DynamicResourceExtension  
    • TemplateBindingExtension  

    When XAML parser sees:
    {Binding Name}
    it creates:
    new Binding("Name");

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    5. XAML Builds the Logical Tree First
    Logical Tree = structure of your UI.

    Window
     └── Grid
           ├── TextBlock
           └── Button

    Logical Tree controls:
    • Resource lookup  
    • DataContext inheritance  
    • Event routing (bubbling/tunneling)  
    • Styles  

    Whenever you place controls inside Panels, Logical Tree is what WPF uses to find parents.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    6. Then WPF Builds the Visual Tree
    Visual Tree contains the REAL rendered elements.
    A Button becomes:

    Button
     └── Border
         └── ContentPresenter
             └── TextBlock (“Click me”)  

    The Visual Tree is detailed, containing:
    • borders
    • presenters
    • decorators
    • visuals
    • shapes

    Logical Tree = your layout  
    Visual Tree = what actually gets drawn  

    Visual Tree is crucial for:
    • rendering
    • hit testing
    • triggers
    • animations
    • templates

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    7. Complete Internal Pipeline of XAML Loading

    YourWindow.xaml  
        ↓ Compile  
    YourWindow.baml  
        ↓ Runtime  
    BamlReader  
        ↓  
    XamlParser  
        ↓  
    ObjectWriter (creates .NET objects)  
        ↓  
    Dependency properties applied  
        ↓  
    Logical Tree built  
        ↓  
    Control Templates applied  
        ↓  
    Visual Tree built  
        ↓  
    PresentationCore  
        ↓  
    MILCore  
        ↓  
    DirectX  
        ↓  
    Screen  

    This is the *real* internal operation of WPF.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    8. Why XAML Must Be Compiled Into BAML
    Reasons:
    • Faster to load  
    • Pre-parsed  
    • Tokenized  
    • Smaller size  
    • Better startup performance  
    • Avoids runtime XML parsing  
    • Helps WPF designer load faster  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    9. XAML &amp; C# Compilation Together
    WPF adds only ONE extra step:
    XAML → BAML

    Everything else is the same as any .NET program:
    C# → IL → JIT → Machine code

    So WPF is not “special” at runtime.
    It simply has an additional build step to convert markup into binary.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Section Summary

    ✔ XAML compiles into BAML  
    ✔ BAML is loaded by the XAML engine  
    ✔ .NET objects are created dynamically  
    ✔ Dependency properties are applied  
    ✔ Logical Tree and Visual Tree are constructed  
    ✔ Templates and styles get applied  
    ✔ Rendering goes through DirectX  

    Once you understand this, WPF’s “magic” becomes predictable and logical.

