    Dependency Properties (DPs)
    Dependency Properties are the MOST important concept in WPF.  
    If you master Dependency Properties, then:
    • Binding  
    • Styling  
    • Animation  
    • Templates  
    • Resources  
    • Attached properties  
    • Inheritance  
    • Commands (indirectly)  
    all become intuitive.

    DPs power nearly EVERYTHING in WPF.  
    They exist because normal .NET properties are not enough for a UI framework.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    1. Why Normal .NET Properties Are Not Enough
    A regular C# property is:
    • a private field  
    • with getter and setter  
    • holding a value  
    • no metadata  
    • no change notifications  
    • no support for binding  
    • no animations  
    • no default values  
    • no template integration  

    WPF needed properties that support:
    ✔ Binding  
    ✔ Animation  
    ✔ Styling  
    ✔ Inheritance  
    ✔ Value precedence  
    ✔ Templates  
    ✔ Resources  
    ✔ Change notifications  
    ✔ High-performance storage  

    Therefore, WPF invented Dependency Properties.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    2. What Is a Dependency Property?
    A Dependency Property (DP) is:
    ✔ Stored in WPF’s internal property store (not a field)  
    ✔ Has metadata  
    ✔ Supports styles  
    ✔ Supports animations  
    ✔ Supports templates  
    ✔ Supports binding  
    ✔ Supports value inheritance  
    ✔ Supports default values  
    ✔ Supports callbacks  
    ✔ Supports coercion  
    ✔ Supports change notifications  

    It is a SUPER property system, 10x more powerful than regular properties.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    3. The DependencyObject Class (Where DPs Live)
    Object  
        ↓  
    DispatcherObject  
        ↓  
    DependencyObject  
        ↓  
    UIElement  
        ↓  
    FrameworkElement  
        ↓  
    Control  
        ↓  
    (Button, ListBox, TextBox, etc.)

    Every WPF control inherits from DependencyObject.  
    This class contains:
    • the global property store  
    • GetValue() and SetValue()  
    • logic for metadata  
    • value resolution  
    • change notifications  
    • dependency system core  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    4. How a Dependency Property Is Registered  
    Example:

    public static readonly DependencyProperty AgeProperty =
        DependencyProperty.Register(
            "Age",
            typeof(int),
            typeof(PersonControl),
            new PropertyMetadata(0));

    Wrapper property:

    public int Age
    {
        get => (int)GetValue(AgeProperty);
        set => SetValue(AgeProperty, value);
    }

    This registers:
    • name of the property  
    • type  
    • owner  
    • default value  
    • metadata  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    5. The MOST Important Feature — Property Value Precedence
    When WPF needs the value of a DP, it checks in this order:

    1. Animation (highest priority)  
    2. Local value (XAML or SetValue)  
    3. TemplatedParent value  
    4. Style triggers  
    5. Template setters  
    6. Style setters  
    7. Inherited value  
    8. Default value (metadata)  

    This is why:
    • animations override everything  
    • setting a property in XAML overrides styles  
    • styles override defaults  
    • parents can pass values to children  

    No other .NET UI framework has this.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    6. DP Enables Binding
    Binding requires:
    • unified storage  
    • metadata  
    • change notifications  
    • expressions  
    • delayed evaluation  
    • inheritance  

    Normal properties cannot support this.  
    DPs solve all this.

    That is why:

    &lt;TextBlock Text="{Binding Name}"/&gt;

    works magically.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    7. DP Enables Animation
    Because WPF stores DP values internally, animation system can:
    • animate per frame  
    • override values temporarily  
    • apply easing  
    • revert after animation  

    A normal property cannot be animated at all.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    8. DP Enables Styling and Templates
    A style sets default values:

    &lt;Style TargetType="Button"&gt;
        &lt;Setter Property="FontSize" Value="20"/&gt;
    &lt;/Style&gt;

    A template completely replaces visual appearance:

    &lt;ControlTemplate TargetType="Button"&gt;
        ...  
    &lt;/ControlTemplate&gt;

    This is possible ONLY because DP has:
    • metadata  
    • precedence rules  
    • owner types  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    9. Attached Properties (Very Important)
    This XAML:

    Grid.Row="2"
    Canvas.Left="150"

    These properties belong to Grid or Canvas, not the control.

    Attached Property = DP that you attach to other elements.

    Used for:
    • layout  
    • behaviors  
    • parent-child communication  
    • attached behaviors  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    10. DP Storage Mechanism (Internal)
    Each DependencyObject has a hidden table:
    DependencyPropertyEntry[]

    For each DP, WPF stores:
    • effective value  
    • base value  
    • animated value  
    • value source  
    • metadata  
    • flags  

    This system is extremely optimized for UI performance.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    11. DP Metadata
    Metadata can define:
    • default value  
    • callbacks  
    • change notifications  
    • validation  
    • AffectsRender  
    • AffectsMeasure  
    • AffectsArrange  
    • Inheritance  

    Example:

    new FrameworkPropertyMetadata(
        10,
        FrameworkPropertyMetadataOptions.AffectsMeasure |
        FrameworkPropertyMetadataOptions.AffectsArrange,
        OnSizeChanged);

    This means:
    • if value changes → re-measure layout  
    • call OnSizeChanged  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    12. Why DP Is the Heart of WPF
    DP powers:
    • Binding  
    • Animation  
    • Styling  
    • Templates  
    • Resources  
    • Inheritance  
    • Attached properties  
    • Layout system  

    WPF literally could not exist without DPs.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Section Summary  
    Dependency Properties provide:
    ✔ Superior storage model  
    ✔ Metadata-driven behavior  
    ✔ Binding  
    ✔ Animation  
    ✔ Styling  
    ✔ Inheritance  
    ✔ Attached properties  
    ✔ Optimized performance  

    It is the ONE system that makes WPF more powerful than ANY other .NET UI framework.

