    Routed Events (How Events Travel in WPF)
    Routed Events are one of the biggest strengths of WPF.
    They allow events to travel through the UI tree instead of staying inside one control like normal .NET events.

    This makes WPF:
    • More flexible  
    • More powerful  
    • Better for templating  
    • Better for MVVM  
    • Better for central event handling  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    1. Why Normal .NET Events Are Not Enough
    In normal .NET (WinForms style events):

    button.Click += OnClick;

    Problems:
    • Event stays inside the button  
    • Parent controls cannot react  
    • No preview events  
    • No way to intercept input  
    • No routing  
    • No unified input system  

    Modern UI frameworks need:
    ✔ parent panels to react  
    ✔ controls to intercept global input  
    ✔ ability to bubble/tunnel  
    ✔ preview events  
    ✔ template events  

    So WPF introduced Routed Events.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    2. What Is a Routed Event?
    A Routed Event is an event that can travel along the UI tree.

    It can travel in 3 ways:
    1. Bubbling (Child → Parent → Window)  
    2. Tunneling (Window → Parent → Child)  
    3. Direct (stays on element but uses routed event system)  

    This allows:
    • parent controls to react to child events  
    • global input handling  
    • preview events  
    • event interception  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    3. Types of Routed Events

    1) Bubbling Events (Most Common)
    Flow:
    Child → Parent → Window

    Example: Button.Click
    If user clicks a Button inside a Grid:
    • Button handles it  
    • Grid receives Click  
    • Window receives Click  

    This allows parent panels to react even if children originate event.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    2) Tunneling Events (Preview Events)
    Flow:
    Window → Panel → Child  
    (opposite direction)

    These events have “Preview” prefix:
    • PreviewMouseDown  
    • PreviewTextInput  
    • PreviewKeyDown  

    Example:
    User clicks a Button.

    Order:
    • Window.PreviewMouseDown  
    • Grid.PreviewMouseDown  
    • Button.PreviewMouseDown  
    (Only after that)
    • Button.MouseDown  

    Parents can intercept input BEFORE children.

    Uses:
    ✔ Key shortcuts  
    ✔ Blocking input  
    ✔ Gesture interception  
    ✔ Custom behavior  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    3) Direct Events
    These behave like normal events:
    • MouseEnter  
    • MouseLeave  
    • LayoutUpdated  

    They still use routed event infrastructure but do not travel.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    4. Why Routed Events Exist (Design Reason)

    They solve these problems:

    ✔ Problem 1  
    Parent controls need to know child activity  
    Example: ListBox should react to clicks inside DataTemplate.

    ✔ Problem 2  
    We need PREVIEW events before child gets input  
    For filtering keys, blocking gestures, hotkeys, etc.

    ✔ Problem 3  
    Templates expand controls  
    A Button is not just a Button.
    It contains Border → ContentPresenter → TextBlock.

    Which element raises Click?
    Routed events fix this.

    ✔ Problem 4  
    MVVM relies on routed commands  
    Commands search up the tree for a handler.

    ✔ Problem 5  
    Unified input system  
    All mouse, keyboard, touch input flows through routed events.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    5. How Routed Events Work Internally

    When an event is fired:

    1) WPF determines the event source  
    (e.g., inside a TextBlock inside a Button inside a Grid)

    2) EventRoute is created  
    This is a list of all elements in the route.

    3) If Tunneling  
    Route = Window → Grid → Button → actual element  
    Raise Preview event from top to bottom.

    4) Then if Bubbling  
    Route = Child → Button → Grid → Window  
    Raise normal event bottom to top.

    5) Each element can:  
    • handle the event  
    • stop routing  
    • modify event args  
    • block propagation (Handled = true)

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    6. Bubbling Example

    &lt;Grid MouseDown="Grid_MouseDown"&gt;
        &lt;Button MouseDown="Button_MouseDown"&gt;Click&lt;/Button&gt;
    &lt;/Grid&gt;

    User clicks the Button.

    Call order:
    1. Button_MouseDown  
    2. Grid_MouseDown  
    3. Window_MouseDown  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    7. Tunneling (Preview) Example

    &lt;Grid PreviewMouseDown="Grid_PreviewMouseDown"&gt;
        &lt;Button PreviewMouseDown="Button_PreviewMouseDown"&gt;Click&lt;/Button&gt;
    &lt;/Grid&gt;

    Call order:
    1. Window PreviewMouseDown  
    2. Grid PreviewMouseDown  
    3. Button PreviewMouseDown  
    4. Button MouseDown  
    5. Grid MouseDown  
    6. Window MouseDown  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    8. Blocking an Event
    Any handler can stop the entire chain:

    e.Handled = true;

    Uses:
    • disable interaction  
    • block mouse or keyboard  
    • custom behavior  
    • prevent event from reaching templates  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    9. Routed Events and Commands
    WPF commands (RoutedCommand, ICommand) rely on routed events to:

    • bubble up  
    • find a handler in parent element  
    • check CanExecute  
    • execute Save, Undo, Open commands  

    This allows MVVM to work cleanly.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    10. Routed vs Traditional Events

    Traditional Events:
    • stay inside the element  
    • do not bubble  
    • do not tunnel  
    • not suitable for templated UI  
    • no global input handling  

    Routed Events:
    • travel through UI tree  
    • support preview (tunneling)  
    • support bubbling  
    • work through templates  
    • enable centralized handling  
    • integrate with commands  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Section Summary
    ✔ Routed Events allow events to travel  
    ✔ Bubbling and Tunneling allow complex UI logic  
    ✔ Input system (mouse, keyboard, touch) uses routed events  
    ✔ Commands depend on routed events  
    ✔ Event routing works with templates and visual tree  
    ✔ You can intercept or cancel input  

    Routed Events are one of the strongest parts of WPF’s architecture.

