            <Expander Header="5. SDO_GEOMETRY in EF Core (Why It Fails &amp; How To Work With It)" IsExpanded="False" Margin="0 10 0 10">
                <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
                    <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" FontSize="14" xml:space="preserve">

    Why can't I use LINQ to retrieve SDO_GEOMETRY directly?
    Because Entity Framework Core does not know how to convert SDO_GEOMETRY into a .NET type.

    Reason:
    EF Core can only map data types that the provider knows how to translate into C#.

    Oracle spatial type:
    MDSYS.SDO_GEOMETRY

    is NOT simple. It contains:
    • nested structures  
    • arrays  
    • optional elements  
    • multiple geometry types  
    • complex rules  

    EF Core cannot map this to:
    • int  
    • string  
    • DateTime  
    • bool  

    Therefore:
    EF Core cannot translate SDO_GEOMETRY into a C# model.

    So LINQ fails with:
    var data = await context.Shapes.ToListAsync();

    Because EF does not know how to map the Geometry column to your Shape property.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    What happens behind the scenes?
    Oracle sends SDO_GEOMETRY as multiple fields:
    • SDO_GTYPE (NUMBER)  
    • SDO_SRID (NUMBER)  
    • SDO_POINT (a nested object)  
    • SDO_ELEM_INFO (array)  
    • SDO_ORDINATES (array)  

    In C# this would require:
    • custom C# classes  
    • serialization code  
    • geometry math  
    • converters  
    • spatial functions  

    Oracle&apos;s EF provider does NOT implement any of this.

    So EF cannot map it.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    If LINQ doesn't work, what is the official solution?
    Use Raw SQL + TO_WKTGEOMETRY().

    Example:

    var result = context.Shapes
        .FromSqlRaw(@"
            SELECT Id, Name,
            SDO_UTIL.TO_WKTGEOMETRY(Shape) AS ShapeWkt
            FROM Shapes")
        .ToListAsync();

    Oracle converts geometry → text format (WKT):

    Examples:
    POINT (10 20)
    LINESTRING (10 10, 60 60)
    POLYGON ((...))

    WKT = Well Known Text  
    A universal geometry format.

    EF Core can map this easily because it is a string.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Alternative 1: Use NetTopologySuite (Recommended)
    NetTopologySuite converts WKT into geometry objects.

    Flow:
    1. From Oracle → get WKT string  
    2. Use NTS reader  
    3. Use geometry object in C#  

    Example:

    var geom = new WKTReader().Read(shape.ShapeWkt);

    You now have a geometry with:
    • Area  
    • Distance  
    • Contains  
    • Intersects  
    • Buffer  
    • Centroid  
    • Envelope  

    Everything works using NTS, free of cost.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Alternative 2: PostgreSQL + PostGIS (BEST Spatial DB)
    If you are not forced to use Oracle, PostGIS is:
    • fully supported  
    • free  
    • EF Core integrates automatically  
    • can use LINQ  
    • maps geometry types natively  
    • uses NTS internally  

    Example EF model:

    public Point Location { get; set; }
    public Polygon Boundary { get; set; }

    EF maps these automatically.

    No hacks needed.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Why Oracle doesn&apos;t support SDO_GEOMETRY in EF?
    Because Oracle&apos;s EF Core provider:
    • focuses on legacy enterprise DB apps  
    • slow development  
    • no spatial mapping  
    • lags behind modern EF features  

    Meanwhile:
    • PostGIS provider is open source and actively maintained  
    • SQL Server spatial works out of the box  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Feature Comparison Table

    Feature             Oracle-SDO       PostGIS          SQL Server
    LINQ support        No               Yes              Yes
    EF native support   No               Yes              Yes
    Raw SQL required    Yes              No               No
    Free                Yes              Yes              Yes
    NTS compatibility   Works via WKT    Full native      Full native

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    So what should YOU do?

    If using Oracle:
    ✔ Use Raw SQL + WKT + NetTopologySuite  

    If learning spatial:
    ✔ Use PostgreSQL + PostGIS (best experience)

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Quick Summary
    • EF Core cannot map SDO_GEOMETRY  
    • LINQ fails because provider lacks spatial mapping  
    • Raw SQL + WKT is the official workaround  
    • NTS converts WKT to usable geometry objects  
    • PostGIS offers full native support  
    • Oracle&apos;s EF provider is incomplete for spatial data

