            <!-- ==========================
                 SECTION 6: Dispatcher
            =========================== -->
            <Expander Header="6. Dispatcher (UI Thread Manager in WPF)" IsExpanded="False" Margin="0 10 0 10">
                <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
                    <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" FontSize="14" xml:space="preserve">

    What is the Dispatcher?
    Every WPF application has only one UI thread.
    This UI thread is responsible for:
    • drawing UI  
    • updating UI  
    • handling click events  
    • running animations  
    • processing window messages  

    If this thread is blocked:
    • UI freezes  
    • window becomes unresponsive  
    • progress bars stop  
    • no interaction works  

    So WPF introduces:

    Dispatcher = The message queue manager for the UI thread.

    It handles:
    • scheduling UI updates  
    • queuing messages  
    • enforcing the "UI thread only" rule  
    • allowing background threads to request UI updates  

    Think of Dispatcher as:
    A receptionist who controls who gets to talk to the UI thread.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Why do we need Dispatcher?

    Because WPF enforces Thread Affinity:

    "UI elements can ONLY be accessed by the thread that created them."

    That thread = the UI thread.

    If a background thread tries:
    label.Content = "Hello";

    WPF throws:
    InvalidOperationException: The calling thread cannot access this object because a different thread owns it.

    Why?
    Because background threads are NOT allowed to touch UI.

    So the correct way is:

    Dispatcher.Invoke(() =&gt; label.Content = "Hello");

    You tell the Dispatcher:
    "Please ask the UI thread to run this code."

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Internal working model of Dispatcher (simple mental model)
    Imagine:
    UI Thread = a worker  
    Dispatcher = a queue  

    When you call:
    Dispatcher.BeginInvoke(() =&gt; UpdateLabel());

    Steps:
    1. The delegate is placed inside the Dispatcher queue  
    2. UI thread finishes current work  
    3. UI thread pulls the next item from Dispatcher  
    4. UI thread executes the delegate  
    5. UI gets updated safely  

    This keeps the UI thread responsive and safe.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Why background threads cannot touch UI?

    Because:
    • WPF rendering engine  
    • Dependency Property engine  
    • MILCore  
    • Visual Tree  
    • DirectX compositor  

    ALL depend on strict single-thread UI rules.

    Allowing multiple threads to modify UI:
    • breaks rendering  
    • corrupts visual state  
    • causes race conditions  
    • creates deadlocks  

    So WPF enforces one-thread UI.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    When do you need the Dispatcher?

    Use Dispatcher when you are:
    ✔ on a background thread  
    ✔ and you want to update UI  

    Examples:
    • update Label  
    • add items to ObservableCollection incorrectly  
    • change TextBox  
    • modify visuals  
    • navigate Frame  
    • show a MessageBox  

    Not needed when:
    • you are already inside a UI event (Click, Loaded, etc.)  
    • code is executed naturally on UI thread  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Dispatcher vs Thread vs Task vs SynchronizationContext

    Thread  
    • raw OS-level worker  
    • heavy  

    ThreadPool  
    • pool of reusable worker threads  

    Task  
    • abstraction over ThreadPool  
    • supports async/await  

    Dispatcher  
    • manages UI thread  

    SynchronizationContext  
    • general abstraction  
    • WPF uses one tied to Dispatcher  

    So when you write:
    await Task.Delay(1000);

    Execution automatically returns to UI thread (unless ConfigureAwait(false) is used).

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Example: Long running work without freezing UI

    Wrong way (block UI):
    private void Button_Click(...)
    {
        Thread.Sleep(3000); // UI freeze
    }

    Correct way:
    private async void Button_Click(...)
    {
        StatusLabel.Content = "Loading...";

        await Task.Run(() =&gt;
        {
            Thread.Sleep(3000); // background
        });

        StatusLabel.Content = "Done!";
    }

    Why no Dispatcher?
    Because async/await automatically returns control to UI thread.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Using Dispatcher explicitly

    Background thread:
    Task.Run(() =&gt;
    {
        var msg = "Finished";

        Application.Current.Dispatcher.Invoke(() =&gt;
        {
            StatusLabel.Content = msg;
        });
    });

    Without this:
    ❌ Cross-thread access exception.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    What happens if you dont use Dispatcher?

    Task.Run(() =&gt; StatusLabel.Content = "Hi");

    This throws exception because:
    StatusLabel belongs to the UI thread.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Dispatcher Priorities
    Dispatcher supports different priorities:
    • Send (highest)  
    • Normal  
    • Background  
    • ApplicationIdle  
    • ContextIdle  
    • SystemIdle  

    Example:
    Dispatcher.BeginInvoke(
        DispatcherPriority.Background,
        new Action(() =&gt; StatusText = "Updated")
    );

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    DispatcherTimer
    Used for periodic UI updates.

    var timer = new DispatcherTimer();
    timer.Interval = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1);
    timer.Tick += (s,e) =&gt; Clock.Text = DateTime.Now.ToString();
    timer.Start();

    Runs on UI thread automatically.

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Real-world scenario where Dispatcher saves UI

    You load 10,000 shapes on a map.
    Background thread loads geometry.
    Dispatcher adds shapes to UI safely:

    Dispatcher.Invoke(() =&gt;
    {
        MapCanvas.Children.Add(polygon);
    });

    If you skip Dispatcher:
    • app crashes  
    • UI corrupts  
    • random access violations  

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    When NOT to use Dispatcher

    Do NOT run heavy work inside Dispatcher:

    Wrong:
    Dispatcher.Invoke(HeavyWork); // runs on UI thread, freezes UI

    Correct:
    await Task.Run(HeavyWork);

    ------------------------------------------------------------

    Summary:

    Concept                  Meaning
    UI Thread                The only thread allowed for UI work
    Dispatcher               Manages UI thread job queue
    BeginInvoke              Queue UI work (async)
    Invoke                   Run on UI thread (sync)
    async/await              Safe way to return to UI thread
    Task.Run                 Move heavy work to background thread

    Dispatcher is the backbone of WPF threading model.

